  "usbFunction": {
    "comment": "The XUSB functions: the USB devices the profiles whose xinput.via is xusbCompanion present on the USB/IP backend, which Windows gives its inbox XUSB driver, xusb22.sys. A profile presents the first function whose when its data meets. refusedIds are identities no function takes.",
    "functions": [
      {
        "name": "receiver",
        "comment": "The Xbox 360 Wireless Receiver for Windows, 045E:0719, with one wireless pad connected on its first slot: the USB device a profile whose connection is wireless-adapter presents, which Windows gives xusb22 by the hardware ID USB\\Vid_045E&Pid_0719 (xusb22.inf). The descriptors and strings are a retail receiver's, as the lsusb dump of rombert's gist 6902ab691e12ab478e31 records them: bcdDevice 1.00, a vendor-class device with eight interfaces, a controller interface (FF/5D/81) and a voice interface (FF/5D/82) for each of four slots, each with its XUSB adapter descriptor (type 0x22, [MS-XUSBI] 2.2.5 and 3.5.1.1.2), bus powered with remote wakeup at 260 mA, and no Microsoft OS descriptor. fields write the receiver's identity from the profile's xinput.information. The pad behind it is the profile: its 29-byte data report ([MS-XUSBI] 3.5.5.1.1, type 0x01) carries the wired pad's report after the header 00 01 00 F0, the form tattiebogle.net's Wireless Gaming Receiver Information page captured (000100F00013...), xpad.c's xpad360w_process_packet reads at byte 4 and 360Controller's WirelessHIDDevice::receivedMessage hands on from byte 4. Once configured the first slot reports its pad attached, 08 80 ([MS-XUSBI] 3.5.5.1.3), then the pad's link control packet (type 0x0F, 3.5.5.1.1.4), the bytes tattiebogle.net captured from a retail pad with the device ID at byte 10 taken from the controller's serial (360Controller reads the serial there) and the profile's subtype at byte 25. Byte 16 is 0x13, the battery code tattiebogle.net and 360Controller name, and the level after it 0xE7, as captured. A connection request (08, [MS-XUSBI] 3.5.5.2.3; xpad.c xpad_inquiry_pad_presence sends 08 00 0F C0) is answered the same way on the first slot and with 08 00, nothing attached, on the other three. outputPackets turn the receiver's rumble request (00 01 0F C0 00 big small, xpad.c and tattiebogle.net) and LED request (00 00 08 40+pattern) into the companion's packets, as the wired function does. The power-off request 00 00 08 C0 (xpad.c xpad360w_poweroff_controller) is taken and the pad stays connected: the persona has no power button to bring it back. Not emulated: voice, the chatpad and plug-in modules, the battery level falling, and pads on the other three slots.",
        "when": { "connection": "wireless-adapter" },
