      {
        "name": "wired",
        "comment": "The XUSB function: the USB device every xusbCompanion profile presents on the USB/IP backend, which Windows gives its inbox XUSB driver, xusb22.sys, by the hardware ID USB\\Vid_045E&Pid_028E or the compatible ID USB\\MS_COMP_XUSB10 (xusb22.inf). The descriptors and strings are a retail wired Xbox 360 controller's, 045E:028E bcdDevice 0x0114, as ControllersInfo's xbox360/DescriptorDump_Controller (Xbox 360 Wired Model X854237-001).txt records them: a vendor-class device with four interfaces, the game controller interface (FF/5D/01, interrupt IN 0x81 and OUT 0x01), the headset interface (FF/5D/03), the plug-in module interface (FF/5D/02) and the security interface (FF/FD/13), and no Microsoft OS descriptor. The retail pad has all four, and xusb22 computes XInputGetCapabilities' voice and plug-in module flags from what the device presents. fields write the profile's VID and PID into the device descriptor and its resolved xinput.subType into byte 4 of the XUSB interface descriptor ([MS-XUSBI] 2.2.2.1.1 and 2.2.4.5), configuration byte 22. String 3 is the controller's own serial. report is the 20-byte input report of [MS-XUSBI] 3.2.5.1.1, which SubmitState fills from the same six stick and trigger slots and the same buttons the XUSB companion's slice carries on the UMDF2 backend, so XInputGetState reads the same on both. outputPackets turn the two output reports xusb22 sends, rumble ([MS-XUSBI] 3.2.5.2.1) and the LED request (3.2.5.2.2), into the 5-byte IOCTL_XUSB_SET_STATE buffer xinput1_4 hands the companion on UMDF2 (device index, LED, left motor, right motor, flags 2 for vibration or 1 for the LED, OpenXInput src/OpenXinput.cpp InSetState_t, SendDeviceVibration and SendLEDState), published with source XInput as driver/companion.c publishes it. The interfaces other than the XUSB one park their reads and take their writes, as a retail pad does with nothing plugged in.",
